import json
from pathlib import Path
p=Path(__file__).parent;r=json.loads((p/'reviews.json').read_text())
urls={'Junior':'https://cdn.b3web.xyz/web/cms/optimizedAEpilogisGreek-Solutions-Juniors.pdf1738668991.pdf','Senior':'https://cdn.b3web.xyz/web/cms/optimizedA_Epil_Megaloi_2024_Greek_solutions.pdf1738669121.pdf'}
rows={'Junior':[(1.5,'Combinatorics','Each choice of the first nine digits has exactly one possible last digit.',None,1),(3.5,'Geometry','The perpendicular bisector passes through the square centre; central symmetry gives the parallel lines.',None,1),(3.5,'Algebra','Use the constraint to factor each radicand; their product becomes a rational square.',None,2),(4,'Number theory','Factor the smaller prime cubed plus one; the larger prime squared forces divisibility of the linear factor.',r'Find all positive integers $r$ expressible as $$r=\frac{p^3+q^3+1}{p^2q^2}$$ for primes $p,q$.',3)],'Senior':[(2.5,'Algebra','Three iterations give the identity map, so the large iterate collapses.',None,1),(2.5,'Combinatorics','The equilateral vertex triples are disjoint, with six admissible colourings each.',None,2),(6.5,'Geometry','Several reflected radius relations yield two linked triangle similarities, then a cyclic angle finishes the second claim.',r'In acute $ABC$, let $D,E,F$ be the altitude feet from $A,B,C$, and $O$ the circumcentre. The tangents at $B,C$ meet at $T$. Reflect $O$ in $AB,AC$ to obtain $K,L$, and reflect $T$ in $BC$ to obtain $S$. Put $N=SL\cap BC$. Prove (a) $\angle KSF=\angle ESL$; (b) $D,E,L,N$ are concyclic.',2),(4.5,'Number theory','Separate unequal exponents using exponential growth, then trap an integer cube between consecutive cubes.',r'Find all integers $m,n$ satisfying $$2^{n^6+7}+m^6=(n^6+7)^2+2^{m^3}.$$' ,5)]}
for lev,rr in rows.items():
 for n,(v,t,why,st,pg) in enumerate(rr,1):
  slug=f'cyp_2025_selection1_{lev.lower()}_{n}';src=urls[lev];su=src+f'#page={pg}';sol='[Official worked solution (Greek)]('+su+').';kind='source-link'
  if lev=='Junior' and n==4:
   sol=r'The answer is $r=1$, obtained from $\{p,q\}=\{2,3\}$. Assume $p\ge q$. The equation implies $p^2\mid(q+1)(q^2-q+1)$. If $p$ did not divide $q+1$, it would follow that $p^2\mid q^2-q+1$, impossible because this positive number is less than $p^2$. Thus $p\mid q+1$. Since $q\le p$, this forces $p=q+1$. The only consecutive primes are 2 and 3, and substitution gives $r=1$.';kind='original-exposition'
  if lev=='Senior' and n==4:
   sol=r'If $m<0$, then $2^{m^3}$ is a nonintegral rational while every other term is integral, impossible. Put $a=n^6+7\ge7$ and $b=m^3\ge0$. The equation is $2^a-2^b=a^2-b^2$. If $a\ne b$, let $u=\max(a,b)$ and $v=\min(a,b)$. Then $2^{u-1}\le2^u-2^v=u^2-v^2\le u^2$, contradicting $2^{u-1}>u^2$ for every integer $u\ge7$ (proved by induction from $u=7$). Hence $m^3=n^6+7$. Writing $t=n^2$, we have $m\ge t+1$, so $t^3+7\ge(t+1)^3$, or $t^2+t\le2$. Thus $t=0$ or $1$. The former gives no integer cube; the latter gives $m=2$. Therefore $(m,n)=(2,1),(2,-1)$.';kind='original-exposition'
  (p/(slug+'.json')).write_text(json.dumps({'country':'Cyprus','competition':'Cyprus Mathematical Society Selection Competition','year':2025,'section':'Selection Test A — '+lev,'problem_number':str(n),'problem_markdown':st or 'See the official paper for the full statement.','solutions_markdown':[]},ensure_ascii=False,indent=2))
  r[slug]={'difficulty':v,'topic':t,'tags':[],'reason':why,'number':str(n),'sourceUrl':src,'solutionUrl':su,'solution':sol,'solutionKind':kind,'sourceName':'Cyprus Mathematical Society — official paper and worked solutions','license':'Original competition rights retained; concise English mathematical paraphrase.','notes':'Dated 25 January 2025 in the paper; the senior PDF filename retains 2024.'}
(p/'reviews.json').write_text(json.dumps(r,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2))
